  "body": "When ::first-letter has visibility:hidden, browsers behaves differently in innerText and display.\r\nDemo URI https://jsfiddle.net/023yhmfx/5/\r\n```html\r\n<style>#target::first-letter { visibility:hidden; }</style>\r\n<p id=\"target\">abc /p>``\r\n```\r\n- Chrome and Safari: innerText=\"\", display: \"bc\"\r\n- Edge and Firefox: innerText=\"abc\", display: \"abc\"\r\n\r\nNote: Chrome will change innerText to return \"bc\".\r\n\r\nIt seems root causes is handling of \"visibility\" CSS property in rendering. CSS spec for ::first-letter doesn't mention to apply \"visibility\" but it says:\r\n> User agents may apply other properties as well.\r\nChrome/Safari apply and Edge/Firefox don't.\r\n\r\nNote: ::first-line doesn't apply `visibility:hidden` for ::first-line.\r\n\r\nCan we change step 2 of *inner text collection steps* to\r\n>If node's computed value of 'visibility' is not 'visible', then return items.\r\n>Note: if node is Text node and ::first-letter style, check 'visibility' ::first-letter part \r\n\r\nOr, is it better to make Chrome and Safari not to apply 'visibility' for ::first-letter?\r\n\r\n[1] http://crrev.com/c/1134487 Handle invisible ::first-letter in TextIterator\r\n[2] https://drafts.csswg.org/css-pseudo-4/#first-letter-pseudo",
